import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { FormHero } from "../../demos/form/hero.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Form" subtitle="A native form that validates its fields, waits for the server and shows the errors it returns.">
    <Demo source="form/hero"><FormHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="form/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Form</code> is a native <code>{"<form>"}</code>. Submitting it with a submit button or with{" "}
      <kbd>Enter</kbd> in a field, resetting it with a reset button, and the native submission itself are the
      browser's. Its controls' constraints are checked by the browser's constraint validation, which the{" "}
      <a href="../field/">Fields</a> read.
    </p>
    <p>
      The form carries <code>novalidate</code>, so the browser doesn't stop a submit with its own message bubbles.
      The library validates instead:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>On submit, every enabled Field inside is validated, native constraints first and then <code>validate</code>.</li>
      <li>
        If any is invalid, the submit is prevented, every field shows its error, and focus moves to the first
        invalid field in document order. From then on, fields in the default <code>"onSubmit"</code> mode validate
        on every change.
      </li>
      <li>
        If all are valid, <code>onSubmit</code> is called with the event, then <code>onFormSubmit</code> with the
        values of the named fields. The form waits for it, and shows on their fields the errors it comes back with.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A browser still counts a submit attempt as interaction, so <code>:user-invalid</code> matches the invalid
      controls after one, <code>novalidate</code> or not.
    </p>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="submitting">Submitting</h3>
    <p>
      With <code>onFormSubmit</code>, a valid submit is handed to you: the native submission is prevented and the
      function receives an object of the named fields' values, keyed by each <code>FieldRoot</code>'s{" "}
      <code>name</code>. A text input's value is a string, a checkbox's or switch's a boolean, a radio group's the
      checked value, and a checkbox group's a list. Disabled fields are left out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without <code>onFormSubmit</code>, a valid submit goes ahead natively, to the form's <code>action</code>, and
      sends what the browser sends: the named controls' values as form data. <code>onSubmit</code> is called first
      in both cases, and only for a valid submit.
    </p>

    <h3 id="pending">Waiting for the server</h3>
    <p>
      <code>onFormSubmit</code> may take its time: return a promise, or write it as an action that{" "}
      <code>await</code>s, as the hero does. Until it ends the form carries <code>data-submitting</code> and{" "}
      <code>aria-busy="true"</code>, and a second submit is ignored, so one request can't race another. Style the
      pending state from the form: the hero's button swaps its label with{" "}
      <code>.Form[data-submitting] .Submit</code>. The fields stay live meanwhile; what someone types while the
      request is out registers at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fake server in the hero answers after a little over a second. It is a small JavaScript module,{" "}
      <code>server.js</code>, declared to the demo by a one-line <code>server.d.rx</code>, as any library call is.
    </p>

    <h3 id="server-errors">Errors from the server</h3>
    <p>
      What <code>onFormSubmit</code> resolves to, if anything, is the errors the server found: an object of
      messages (or lists of messages) keyed by field name. Each is shown by its field's <code>FieldError</code> and
      makes the field invalid, and focus moves to the first field that has one. An error is hidden once its
      field's value changes, and a field that still has one blocks the next submit. The same error coming back
      from the next submit shows again.
    </p>
    <p>
      An error is about the value that was sent. If someone edits a field while the request is out, the error the
      server returns for that field is dropped, since the value it was about is gone.
    </p>

    <h3 id="failure">When the request fails</h3>
    <p>
      If <code>onFormSubmit</code> throws, or its promise rejects (the network dropped), the form stops submitting,
      can be submitted again, and calls <code>onFormError</code> with the error. Without{" "}
      <code>onFormError</code> the failure is reported to the page as an uncaught error is: the console,{" "}
      <code>window.onerror</code>, your monitoring.
    </p>
    <p>
      An action that <code>await</code>s must catch its own failures (the compiler insists), so the hero shows its
      message from a <code>catch</code>. <code>onFormError</code> is for a handler that returns a promise it doesn't
      catch:
    </p>
    <Code file="form/promise.rx.txt" />

    <h3 id="errors-prop">Errors from elsewhere</h3>
    <p>
      <code>errors</code> shows errors that didn't come from a submit, such as a page the server rendered with
      them. Each entry makes its field invalid and is hidden once the field's value changes, until{" "}
      <code>errors</code> itself changes.
    </p>

    <h3 id="reset">Resetting</h3>
    <p>
      A reset button, or <code>form.reset()</code>, is the browser's: it puts every control back to its default.
      The controls follow it. An uncontrolled one takes its default as its value and reports the change once; a
      controlled one keeps the value its owner holds. Each Field returns to untouched and unvalidated, with its
      errors cleared, those from the server included, and the form returns to before its first submit. A reset
      isn't a change: it validates nothing.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Select, Combobox, Autocomplete, Slider and Number Field don't take part in a reset yet, and neither does a Field around
        one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3 id="validation-mode">When fields validate</h3>
    <p>
      <code>validationMode</code> sets the mode for every field inside: <code>"onSubmit"</code> (the default),{" "}
      <code>"onBlur"</code> or <code>"onChange"</code>. A field's own <code>validationMode</code> wins. Whatever the
      mode, a submit validates every field. The <a href="../field/">Field</a> page has the details.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="form" />
  </DocsPage>
}
